1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,840
You just added an MCP server

2
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:03,740
to Cursor, and it immediately fails

3
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:06,140
with a red 'spawn ENOENT' error.

4
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:07,980
Yet when you run the exact

5
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:09,620
same command in your terminal, it

6
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:10,200
works fine.

7
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:12,540
Why does Cursor think it doesn't

8
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:12,820
exist?

9
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:16,340
Cursor is a desktop GUI app—it

10
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:18,560
often fails to inherit your terminal's

11
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,440
PATH, or tries to spawn Windows

12
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:23,380
dot-cmd wrappers like npx without an

13
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:23,800
extension.

14
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:25,680
The fix takes ten seconds.

15
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,500
In PowerShell, run 'where.exe uvx' or

16
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,340
'where.exe npx'—don't forget the dot-exe—then paste

17
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:35,400
the full executable path into mcp.json

18
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:37,220
with double backslashes.

19
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:40,980
Save your config, hit Reload, and

20
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:41,240
boom:

21
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:43,600
your MCP server lights up green

22
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,360
with all six tools loaded and

23
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:46,260
ready for your agent.

24
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:48,780
If this saved your build, drop

25
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:50,320
a like and subscribe for more

26
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:51,640
zero-fluff dev fixes.

